    "body": "# Live typing\n\nAdds a toggleable in-editor type feedback mode that fills in statically unknown types from what actually happened at runtime. Where a type is statically unknown, statics has nothing to check against and stays quiet; once runtime fills the unknown in, a genuine misuse becomes reportable.\n\nStacked on #1858, which contributes the machinery for colouring the runtime-derived parts of a type.\n\n## Example\n\n[Screencast From 2025-11-18 13-45-45.webm](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/42d6e2c5-e0f2-4093-b1ef-547807dc26d0)\n\n## Probe selection\n\nIntegrated with the `Probe` pinning system, so you can get dynamic types for one particular function application by pinning it.\n\n[Screencast From 2025-11-18 13-53-21.webm](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/9d9bdec4-12d6-47d8-8fa3-d906989b9bc8)\n\n## What you see\n\n- Live-typing errors get their own decoration in the editor \u2014 a pulsing dashed outline in a colour distinct from ordinary static errors \u2014 so it is clear that an error depends on runtime information.\n- The cursor inspector shows the refined type with the runtime-derived components in green, and a \u26a1 badge in its header when only the dynamic info reports an error.\n- They also collect in the problem panel under a `LiveTyping` category.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- **Probing what statics could not resolve** \u2014 with `live_typing` on, `CachedStatics.compute_targets` unions `ids_with_unknown_types(info_map)` into the probe targets, so every expression and pattern whose `elab_syn_ty` still contains an unknown gets sampled. The feature supplies its own inputs rather than depending on where you happened to place probes.\n- **`LiveTyping.re`** \u2014 carries per-expression dynamic samples and per-TPat type instantiations, consumed by `Statics.mk(~dynamics=...)`. A term's samples are only consulted when its type actually has unknowns to fill.\n- **`CodeWithStatics.re`** \u2014 filters `Dynamics.t` by the current `sample_focus` and runs statics a second time with the dynamic info, surfacing dynamic-only errors as `live_typing_error_ids`. The second pass is throttled during streaming evaluation.\n- **`Dynamics.TypeInstMap` / `EvaluatorState.RecordTypeInstantiation`** \u2014 TypAp transitions record the concrete type at each type-variable instantiation, so polymorphic code gets instantiation-aware feedback instead of a spurious error on the parameter. `(typfun a -> fun x : a -> x)@<String>(\"\")` no longer reports on `x`.\n- **`Typ.join` / `join_all`** \u2014 the lattice dual of `meet`, used by `LiveTyping.extend_ctx_with_instantiations` to combine the observed instantiations of a type variable. Like `meet`, it hands back the alias when the join does not refine it, and it stops rather than looping on a cyclic alias chain.\n- **Closure scoping** \u2014 `DynamicTypInfer.type_of_sample` substitutes a sampled closure's own environment before typing it. Statics discards a closure's env, so the body's free variables would otherwise resolve against the probe site's context and capture whatever same-named binder happens to be in scope there.\n- **Cursor inspector** \u2014 `CursorInspector.view_type` takes `~live_typing_info` and, where the dynamic type differs from the static one, renders the segment and green ids that `DynamicTypInfer.segment_and_dynamic_ids` returns.\n- **Settings** \u2014 `CoreSettings.live_typing`, off by default, toggled from the nut menu with a performance warning.\n\n## Testing\n\n`test/evaluator/Test_Evaluator_LiveTyping.re` covers the end-to-end flow: sample collection, meet across samples, pinned-call filtering, and polymorphic instantiation.\n\n`Test_Typ.re` covers `Typ.join` \u2014 equal atoms, Unknown, sums, unbound variables, alias preservation, cyclic-alias termination. `Test_DynamicTypInfer.re` adds a closure-scoping case, and a check that a type built the way statics builds them colours only its runtime-derived components.\n\n## Known limitations\n\n- `DynamicTypInfer` runs a full `Statics.mk` per sample with no memoization. Tolerable for a single projector in Dynamic mode, more of a concern here, where live typing needs dynamic types for many expressions at once.\n\n\n",
